# Response headers for Cloudflare Workers static assets. Cloudflare reads this
# file from the build output and does not serve it.

# The same content signals as robots.txt. Cloudflare's Markdown for Agents
# copies this header to its Markdown responses. Without it, Cloudflare sends
# its own default, which says ai-train=yes.
/*
  Content-Signal: search=yes, ai-input=yes, ai-train=no
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'

# nosniff: a browser must use the Content-Type that it is sent and must not
# guess another one.
#
# frame-ancestors 'none': no other site may show these pages inside a frame.
# The rest of the Content Security Policy stays in the <meta> tag in
# layouts/partials/extended_head.html, and a browser applies both. A <meta>
# tag cannot carry frame-ancestors, so that one directive is sent as a header.
# The whole policy cannot move here under /*: /sim/a320-ptu/ is a standalone
# page with an inline script, which script-src 'self' would block.

# No long cache lifetime for the hashed files under /js/ and /css/, on
# purpose. Cloudflare applies a rule in this file to a 404 for a matching path
# too. A browser that asks for a new file in the seconds before a deploy has
# reached every Cloudflare location would keep that 404 for the whole
# lifetime. With the default, the browser revalidates each file by its ETag.

# The terminal fetches a page's content from .../index.fragment.html. For one
# hour a browser reuses its copy of a fragment and sends no request. For 30
# days after that, the browser shows its copy at once and checks for a newer
# one in the background. So after a post is edited, a browser that holds the
# old fragment shows it for up to an hour, and then one more time. Cloudflare
# applies this rule to a 404 as well.
/*index.fragment.html
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=2592000

# The images in posts get the same lifetime as the fragments. Without it, a
# browser asks Cloudflare about every image again on every page view. An image
# keeps its URL when it is replaced, so the lifetime is short: after a
# replacement, a browser that holds the old image shows it for up to an hour,
# and then one more time. Cloudflare applies this rule to a 404 as well.
/img/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=2592000
